Beryllium: a canvas snapshot carries the noise getImageData() carries

Purpose: Helium noises a canvas readback with one routine in two kinds of
  place (helium/core/noise/canvas.patch). getImageData() hands it an
  ImageData, whose bytes are red, green, blue, alpha. toDataURL(), toBlob()
  and OffscreenCanvas convertToBlob() hand it a snapshot, read back in the
  snapshot's own colour type where that is one the routine accepts, and it
  accepts blue, green, red, alpha as it stands. The routine hashes a pixel
  as its four bytes and numbers its channels by byte, so on a snapshot in
  that order the hash took blue for the pixel's low byte and "channel 0"
  was blue. One canvas, read on one site in one session, came back with
  two different noises: for a grey fill the same pixels with the step on
  the other of red and blue, for any other content other pixels and other
  steps altogether. A page holding two differently noised copies of an
  image knows more about the image than either copy says, and "the same
  within a session" did not hold across the two calls.
  Measured with the battery (freebsd/privacy/noise-hash/README.md, 11
  November 2026): on Beryllium 0.20.3.17 on FreeBSD the snapshot of a grey
  fill was the pixels of getImageData() with red and blue exchanged in 30
  readbacks of 30, and equal to them only where the step fell on green (10
  of 30). The same on Helium 0.21.2.1 on macOS and Fedora, where the
  snapshot of a textured image was explained by the same five token bits
  once red and blue were exchanged (60 of 60).
  The change: MaybeNoiseSnapshot() reads a blue, green, red snapshot back
  as red, green, blue, as it already does for every colour type the
  routine does not accept. What is encoded afterwards takes either order.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on helium/core/noise/canvas.patch,
  which creates the file.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. First
  built in 0.20.3.18 (released 12 November 2026). This header was brought
  up to date for 0.20.3.19; the code below it is as built.
Test: freebsd/privacy/noise-hash/battery. The rows "toDataURL gives the
  same pixels as getImageData", the same for toBlob and convertToBlob, and
  "Textured image through toDataURL gives the pixels of getImageData" read
  10, 10, 10 and 0 of 30 on 0.20.3.17 and must read 30 of 30. On
  0.20.3.18 (12 November 2026, two runs) they read 30 of 30 each. The
  battery's self-test runs the same rows against stand-in noise that
  behaves as this patch intends. Feature matrix row P-FPR-04.
security-review: not required (changes the byte order a readback is taken
  in; no new input, no new state)

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/helium_noise/canvas_noising_helper.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/core/helium_noise/canvas_noising_helper.cc
@@ -568,6 +568,14 @@
   if (!IsSupportedNoisingColorType(color_type)) {
     color_type = kRGBA_8888_SkColorType;
   }
+  // Beryllium: a blue, green, red snapshot is read back as red, green, blue,
+  // the order of ImageData. The noise hashes a pixel as its four bytes and
+  // numbers its channels by byte, so on a snapshot in the other order it
+  // took blue for red, and toDataURL(), toBlob() and convertToBlob() returned
+  // other noise than getImageData() did for the same canvas.
+  if (color_type == kBGRA_8888_SkColorType) {
+    color_type = kRGBA_8888_SkColorType;
+  }
   auto info = SkImageInfo::Make(
       snapshot->GetSize().width(), snapshot->GetSize().height(), color_type,
       kUnpremul_SkAlphaType, snapshot->GetColorSpace().ToSkColorSpace());
